1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,045
Let's take a break from the theory and spend some time talking about TF records.

2
00:00:06,045 --> 00:00:10,200
TF records are TensorFlow custom data format.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,280
They are not necessarily required to train model with TensorFlow,

4
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,984
but may help when data loading becomes a bottleneck.

5
00:00:17,984 --> 00:00:24,210
Also, the data in the Waymo Open Dataset is using the TFRecord format,

6
00:00:24,210 --> 00:00:27,720
and you will be using that format for your final project.

7
00:00:27,720 --> 00:00:32,280
Whereas XML or JSON format are human readable,

8
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:34,845
TF records files are not.

9
00:00:34,845 --> 00:00:39,790
For example, let's try to open this TF record.

10
00:00:41,410 --> 00:00:45,740
As you can see, this file is not human readable.

11
00:00:45,740 --> 00:00:53,100
However, we can use protofiles to understand the structure of these TF records.

12
00:00:53,230 --> 00:00:59,830
Let's look at an example of a protofile from the Waymo Open Dataset.

13
00:00:59,830 --> 00:01:04,505
This protofile describes the structure of a TF record.

14
00:01:04,505 --> 00:01:06,770
Well, from this profile,

15
00:01:06,770 --> 00:01:11,709
I see that the TF record contains a field called CameraName,

16
00:01:11,709 --> 00:01:16,845
which has the following six attributes; UNKOWN,

17
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:23,505
FRONT, FRONT_LEFT, FRONT_RIGHT, SIDE_LEFT, and SIDE_RIGHT.

18
00:01:23,505 --> 00:01:27,060
Looking at this protofile is extremely informative,

19
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:31,070
and you will have to learn how to leverage them for the final project.

20
00:01:31,070 --> 00:01:36,335
By the way, you can create TF records files from your own data format.

21
00:01:36,335 --> 00:01:42,700
To do so, you will have to leverage a TensorFlow function called tf.train.Example.

22
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:46,065
You will learn about this in the next exercise.

23
00:01:46,065 --> 00:01:49,910
Let's summarize what we have learned about TF records.

24
00:01:49,910 --> 00:01:53,740
TF records are TensorFlow custom data formats.

25
00:01:53,740 --> 00:01:57,469
They are not required to train a model with TensorFlow,

26
00:01:57,469 --> 00:02:00,410
but they may help to speed up data loading.

27
00:02:00,410 --> 00:02:04,000
Their structure is defined by protofiles.

28
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,755
TF records are created using protocol buffers or proto buff,

29
00:02:09,755 --> 00:02:12,960
a mechanism to serialize data.

